'''Zong Balboa Airways''' ({{lang|nan|巴波亞航空}}; ''Pa-po-a Hâng-khong'') was the state-owned airline of the [[Zong Balboa|Dependent State of Balboa and Sant Cristòfor]], operating from [[Sant Cristòfor]] during the period of Zong colonial administration on the [[Balboa Isthmus|isthmus]]. The airline was established in the early 1930s under the governorship of [[Ong Hui-seng]] as part of a broader programme of infrastructure modernisation in the dependent territory. It operated domestic services connecting Sant Cristòfor with the outlying settlements of the territory and international services linking the isthmus to the wider [[Zongosphere]].

The airline's operations were severely disrupted by the outbreak of the [[Great Adonian War]] in 1935 and the [[Balboa Campaign]] that initiated it. During the siege of Sant Cristòfor, which lasted for the duration of the war, the airline's aircraft and facilities suffered extensive damage from aerial bombardment. Operations were reduced to essential supply flights maintained through Zong naval convoy support. The airline's surviving assets were merged into [[Aeroístmus]] in 1948, following the end of the war and the establishment of the [[Adonian Community]] administration on the isthmus.

== History ==

Zong Balboa Airways was established in 1932 under the administration of Governor [[Ong Hui-seng]], as part of a broader programme of infrastructure investment that accompanied the social housing and slum clearance initiatives of the early 1930s. The airline was constituted as a state-owned enterprise under the dependent territory's government, with its operational base at Sant Cristòfor. The {{w|Douglas DC-3}} formed the backbone of the airline's fleet, operating domestic services along the Sant Cristòfor–[[Sant Bart]] corridor and the Shendan coastal route, as well as international connections to destinations in the Zongosphere.

The airline's pre-war route network was oriented toward the connectivity needs of the dependent territory. Domestic services linked Sant Cristòfor with the outlying settlements of the isthmus, while international routes connected the territory to the wider Zong commercial and administrative network. The airline also operated essential services to remote communities, including the islands of the [[Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] and the interior highland settlements, under a public service obligation funded by the territorial administration.

=== Great Adonian War ===

The outbreak of the [[Great Adonian War]] in 1935 and the [[Balboa Campaign]] that opened it placed the airline in an untenable position. The coordinated assault by [[Volisania|Volisan]] and Balboan forces against Zong Balboa targeted Sant Cristòfor and the canal zone in the opening weeks of the war. The canal zone was lost, and the city of Sant Cristòfor was besieged for the duration of the conflict.

During the siege, the airline's facilities at Sant Cristòfor suffered extensive damage from sustained aerial bombardment. Several aircraft were destroyed on the ground or damaged beyond repair. Operations were reduced to a skeletal schedule of essential supply flights, maintained through the Zong naval convoys that sustained the besieged city. The airline's remaining aircraft and equipment were used for emergency transport and supply runs in support of the civil administration under Governor [[Ng Chiau-li]].

=== Dissolution ===

The Great Adonian War ended in 1947 with the collapse of the Volisan fascist government. The siege of Sant Cristòfor was lifted as part of the general cessation of hostilities, but the territory had been devastated: the canal zone was in ruins, the city's infrastructure had suffered extensive damage, and the airline's fleet and facilities were in a state of disrepair.

In 1948, the [[Adonian Community]] assumed responsibility for the post-war administration of the isthmus. The surviving assets of Zong Balboa Airways were merged with those of [[Volisanian Balboa Airlines]] to form [[Aeroístmus]], a new carrier managed by an AC consortium. The merger brought together the remnants of both pre-war airlines under a single administration, ending the separate corporate existence of Zong Balboa Airways.

